 4. 🤖 AI&#x27;s midterm hangover 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet. Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios Americans may have voted in the midterms to tap the AI brakes, but deflating the bubble is a tricky business — as yesterday's market rout showed.

 The big picture: The historical record shows that once major investment booms are in motion, it can be difficult to temper them without sparking a stampede of investors out of assets. That's why market collapses have featured prominently in past investment booms like those in canals, railroads and the internet, Axios markets correspondent Matt Phillips writes . 🚫 Zoom in: The broad rejection of AI in this week's elections — from wins for AI-cautious Democrats to overwhelming support for ballot measures restricting data center construction — has pushed investors to re-evaluate their previous bets on companies profiting from the data center boom.

 Over 50% of the planned data center capacity is in just three states — Ohio, Georgia and Texas. All are places where Democrats flipped governorships. State of play: The tech-heavy Nasdaq fell 5.5% — its worst daily showing since the quasi-panic of President Trump's "liberation day" tariffs in April 2025 — and is down roughly 10% from the high it hit a month ago.

 The Nasdaq 100, a measure of the largest tech stocks, is now down roughly 14% since its Oct. 7 peak. Its 6.4% drop yesterday was its steepest since the March 2020 pandemic panic. Read on . 

 
 
